```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "virtual-references-", fig.width = 8,
                      fig.height = 4, dpi = 120)
```

A model run is usually published as many files: one per month, each holding
that month's days of a four-dimensional array. To analyse the run, those files
have to be seen as the one array they were cut from. There are two ways to do
that without copying any data, and GDAL reads both:

* a **mosaic**: a multidimensional VRT, written by `gdal mdim mosaic`, that
  says which file holds which part of the array and leaves GDAL to open the
  files and read them with the netCDF driver;
* **virtual references**: a Zarr store whose chunks are not stored but
  pointed at, each as a URL, an offset and a length into one of the original
  files. The pointers come from reading the files' internal chunk index once,
  and GDAL reads them with the Zarr driver.

Both end with a `GDALMDArray`, and the values are the same bytes. This article
builds both for the same array and compares them. It follows the
[virtualized](https://github.com/mdsumner/virtualized) repository, which holds
virtual references for the Bluelink reanalysis.

## The data

[BRAN2023](https://research.csiro.au/bluelink/global/reanalysis/) is CSIRO's
Bluelink ocean reanalysis: a global 0.1 degree ocean model run, daily. Its
potential temperature is published on NCI's THREDDS server as one netCDF-4 file
per month, each `(Time, st_ocean, yt_ocean, xt_ocean)` with the days of the
month, 51 depths, 1500 latitudes and 3600 longitudes, stored as packed 16-bit
integers in chunks of one day, one depth and 300 by 300 cells.

THREDDS serves each file over plain HTTP with range requests, so
`/vsicurl/` reads just the parts of a file it needs:

```{r data}
library(GDAL7)

thredds <- "https://thredds.nci.org.au/thredds/fileServer/gb6/BRAN/BRAN2023/daily"
month_url <- function(year, month) {
  sprintf("/vsicurl/%s/ocean_temp_%d_%02d.nc", thredds, year, month)
}
```

Two settings keep GDAL from asking the server for things that are not there: a
directory listing of THREDDS's file area, and the sidecar files (`.aux.xml`,
`.aux` and the rest, in several cases) that GDAL looks for beside any file it
opens and would otherwise ask THREDDS for, one request each.

```{r config}
gdal_config("GDAL_DISABLE_READDIR_ON_OPEN", "EMPTY_DIR")
gdal_config("GDAL_PAM_ENABLED", "NO")
```

## Route one: a mosaic VRT

`gdal mdim mosaic` opens each input, checks that they hold the same array with
the same dimensions, and works out where each one goes from its coordinate
variables. Two months are enough to show it:

```{r mosaic}
inputs <- c(month_url(2024, 11), month_url(2024, 12))
vrt <- file.path(tempdir(), "bran-temp.vrt")
gdal_run("mdim mosaic", list(input = inputs, output = vrt, array = "temp"),
         progress = FALSE)
file.size(vrt)
```

The VRT is a few kilobytes of XML. It describes the array and says which file
holds which days; there are no values in it:

```{r mosaic-xml}
xml <- readLines(vrt)
trimws(grep("SourceFilename|DestSlab", xml, value = TRUE))
```

It opens like any other multidimensional dataset:

```{r mosaic-open}
mosaic_ds <- gdal_open(vrt, multidim = TRUE)
mosaic <- open_mdarray(get_root_group(mosaic_ds), "temp")
mosaic@dimensions
mosaic@block_size
```

The mosaic needed every file opened once to be built: a range request or two
per file for its header, which for the whole run is 180 files. After that, a
read opens only the files it touches.

## Route two: virtual references

The virtualized repository holds a
[kerchunk Parquet](https://fsspec.github.io/kerchunk/spec.html#parquet-references)
reference store for the same variable, built with VirtualiZarr on NCI from the
files in place, and then pointed at the THREDDS URLs. It covers the whole run,
not two months. GDAL's Zarr driver reads a kerchunk Parquet store directly (it
needs GDAL's Parquet driver to read the references), and GitHub serves the
store's files over HTTP like any other:

```{r refs-open}
refs_store <- paste0("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mdsumner/virtualized/",
                     "refs/heads/main/remote/ocean_temp_2023.parq")
refs_ds <- gdal_open(sprintf('ZARR:"/vsicurl/%s"', refs_store), multidim = TRUE)
refs_root <- get_root_group(refs_ds)
refs_root@mdarray_names

refs <- open_mdarray(refs_root, "temp")
refs@dimensions
refs@block_size
```

The chunk shape is the netCDF files' own, because each Zarr chunk is one of
their chunks. Opening read the store's metadata from GitHub and nothing from
THREDDS.

## The same bytes

Pick a day, a depth and a latitude, and read every longitude, from each route.
The two arrays cover different stretches of time, so the day is found by its
value rather than its position. Time is in days since 1979-01-01, at noon:

```{r day}
day <- as.numeric(as.Date("2024-12-01") - as.Date("1979-01-01")) + 0.5
mosaic_t <- match(day, mosaic@dimension_values$Time)
refs_t <- match(day, refs@dimension_values$Time)
c(mosaic = mosaic_t, refs = refs_t)
```

Depth 11 and latitude 101, counting from 1, are the `st_ocean = 10` and
`yt_ocean = 100` of the xarray example in the virtualized README:

```{r same-bytes}
from_mosaic <- read_mdarray(mosaic, start = c(mosaic_t, 11, 101, 1),
                            count = c(1, 1, 1, 3600))
from_refs <- read_mdarray(refs, start = c(refs_t, 11, 101, 1),
                          count = c(1, 1, 1, 3600))
identical(as.vector(from_mosaic), as.vector(from_refs))
```

Identical, as they must be: both read the same compressed chunks of the same
file, one through the netCDF library and one by byte range. The values are the
stored 16-bit integers; the scale and offset turn them into degrees:

```{r unpack}
c(scale = refs@scale, offset = refs@offset)
celsius <- as.vector(from_refs) * refs@scale + refs@offset
head(celsius)
```

The README's xarray read of the same cells gives `-1.54072672, -1.5329445,
-1.52516228, ...`.

## What differs

The values do not, and neither does the chunk shape. The rest does.

**What reading costs.** A read through the mosaic opens the netCDF files it
needs, which means reading each one's HDF5 header and chunk index over HTTP,
and then reads the chunks. A read through the references looks the chunks up
in the Parquet table and asks for their byte ranges directly; the HDF5 index
was read once, when the references were made.

**What building costs.** The mosaic needs only GDAL and the files, and its
build reads every file's header. The references need a tool that understands
the file format's internals (VirtualiZarr or kerchunk in Python, or rhdf5 and
GDAL as in the repository's `zarr/` stores) and a pass over every file.

**Size.** The VRT grows by a few lines per file. The references are one row per
chunk: for this variable that is 5479 days by 51 depths by 60 tiles, about 17
million rows, which is why they are stored as Parquet rather than JSON.

**Metadata.** The mosaic carries the netCDF driver's reading of each file, so
its dimensions have types; the reference store has what was written into its
Zarr metadata:

```{r types}
data.frame(name = mosaic@dimensions$name,
           mosaic = mosaic@dimensions$type,
           refs = refs@dimensions$type)
```

## As a map

Either array is a `GDALMDArray`, so the rest of GDAL7 applies. A view takes one
day at one depth, `advise_read()` fetches its 60 chunks on GDAL's threads, and
`as_classic_dataset()` gives the slice a geotransform from its coordinates:

```{r map}
surface <- get_view(refs, sprintf("[%d,0,:,:]", refs_t - 1))
advise_read(surface, start = c(1, 1), count = c(1500, 3600))
sst <- read_mdarray(surface) * refs@scale + refs@offset
dim(sst)

grid <- as_classic_dataset(surface)
grid@geotransform

lon <- refs@dimension_values$xt_ocean
lat <- refs@dimension_values$yt_ocean
image(lon, lat, sst, asp = 1, useRaster = TRUE,
      col = hcl.colors(64, "Spectral", rev = TRUE), xlab = "", ylab = "")
title("BRAN2023 sea surface temperature, 2024-12-01")
```

Latitude in BRAN runs from south to north, so the first row of this raster is
the southernmost and its pixel height is positive; `image()` takes the
coordinates as they come.

```{r close, include = FALSE}
gdal_close(grid)
gdal_close(mosaic_ds)
gdal_close(refs_ds)
```

## Next

The virtualized repository plans Icechunk stores for the same references.
Icechunk keeps virtual chunk references natively, as a URL, an offset and a
length per chunk, and adds versioning; GDAL reads them through its Icechunk
driver, which turns the URLs into `/vsicurl/` and `/vsis3/` paths the same way.
See [Icechunk](icechunk.html), and [Multidimensional arrays](multidim.html) for
the functions used here.
